1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,850
Okay, but let me ask the question your minister would probably ask.

2
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:05,730
If every government agency is unique,

3
00:00:06,370 --> 00:00:10,150
uh, why can't each one just pick the technical standards that work best for them?

4
00:00:10,910 --> 00:00:18,030
I mean, it might seem easier for one isolated team, but letting each agency pick its own standards breaks interoperability completely.

5
00:00:18,790 --> 00:00:21,650
The solution is what we call a published standards portfolio.

6
00:00:22,030 --> 00:00:25,270
Uh, think of it as a shared menu across the whole of government.

7
00:00:25,690 --> 00:00:27,950
So you aren't inventing new tools from scratch every time.

8
00:00:28,110 --> 00:00:28,570
Exactly.

9
00:00:29,750 --> 00:00:32,090
You are selecting from this published menu.

10
00:00:32,790 --> 00:00:36,150
There are four core technical needs, and we use four published tools to meet them.

11
00:00:36,970 --> 00:00:42,989
Uh, number one is describing services. For this, we use REST with OpenAPI 3.x.

12
00:00:43,890 --> 00:00:48,290
Right, which basically defines how one system can request information from another system.

13
00:00:48,550 --> 00:00:52,710
You have got it. Then number two is proving identity and permissions,

14
00:00:53,410 --> 00:00:57,430
and here the standards are OAuth 2.x and OpenID Connect.

15
00:00:58,150 --> 00:01:04,450
Okay, so that is verifying exactly who you are and, you know, what data you are actually allowed to access.

16
00:01:04,910 --> 00:01:09,370
Yes. And that brings us to number three, which is securing the connection itself.

17
00:01:09,970 --> 00:01:11,870
This requires mutual TLS.

18
00:01:12,650 --> 00:01:15,770
Wait, mutual meaning both ends prove their identity, right?

19
00:01:15,870 --> 00:01:17,370
Not just the user logging in.

20
00:01:18,130 --> 00:01:18,570
Spot on.

21
00:01:19,170 --> 00:01:23,730
Both ends mathematically prove their identity to prevent any imposter from intercepting the data.

22
00:01:24,650 --> 00:01:26,950
And finally, number four is the bus itself.

23
00:01:27,550 --> 00:01:29,370
We use the XRoad message protocol.

24
00:01:30,150 --> 00:01:34,090
Okay, so XRoad connects the systems. We have the connection secure.

25
00:01:34,670 --> 00:01:36,410
But, I mean, connecting the systems is one thing.

26
00:01:36,550 --> 00:01:39,210
How do they actually understand the data traveling through them?

27
00:01:39,730 --> 00:01:41,890
Well, that is where semantic standards come in.

28
00:01:42,450 --> 00:01:51,530
We use ISO/IEC 11179 for consistent data elements and JSON-LD, uh, so the meaning travels right alongside the data.

29
00:01:52,210 --> 00:01:54,790
Plus, we use W3C verifiable credentials.

30
00:01:56,470 --> 00:02:01,850
Let's pause on that last one. How do verifiable credentials actually work in practice for your program?

31
00:02:02,370 --> 00:02:06,630
So using cryptography, a verifier can trust a digital credential immediately,

32
00:02:07,590 --> 00:02:10,650
you know, without ever having to phone the original issuer to check if it is valid.

33
00:02:11,010 --> 00:02:17,690
Oh, wow. So if the education sector issues a student credential now, the health sector can just instantly trust it later.

34
00:02:18,030 --> 00:02:21,310
Precisely. The work done now is seamlessly reused.

35
00:02:22,070 --> 00:02:26,910
But interoperability requires the discipline of everyone choosing those same published standards,

36
00:02:27,590 --> 00:02:30,550
and only whole of government planning can impose that discipline.

37
00:02:31,070 --> 00:02:34,670
You know, a simple procurement clause and a vendor contract just cannot do it.

38
00:02:34,910 --> 00:02:41,230
Right. So this portfolio document, how do we actually document and enforce it across all these different sectors?

39
00:02:41,730 --> 00:02:45,390
The portfolio document is strict, and it must contain four specific parts.

40
00:02:46,090 --> 00:02:49,610
Uh, number one is the standard, its version, and its profile.

41
00:02:50,190 --> 00:02:56,690
And you wanna keep that profile, like your local refinements, as thin as possible, right, so the next sector can adopt it easily?

42
00:02:57,070 --> 00:02:58,550
Yes, exactly. Keep it thin.

43
00:02:59,210 --> 00:03:03,850
Number two is when it binds and how long legacy systems have to comply.

44
00:03:04,490 --> 00:03:07,730
Uh, typically, that is twelve to twenty-four months for a major version.

45
00:03:08,150 --> 00:03:08,690
Makes sense.

46
00:03:09,630 --> 00:03:10,350
Then number three

47
00:03:11,010 --> 00:03:12,890
is how a member proves conformance.

48
00:03:13,510 --> 00:03:15,350
They can't just promise they follow the rules.

49
00:03:15,890 --> 00:03:20,170
They prove it via a self-assessment, a third-party check, or a test suite.

50
00:03:20,610 --> 00:03:22,310
Because without a test, it's just a guess.

51
00:03:22,590 --> 00:03:22,790
Right.

52
00:03:23,870 --> 00:03:25,710
And finally, number four is who keeps it.

53
00:03:26,430 --> 00:03:29,910
The portfolio is actively maintained by the API's working group.

54
00:03:30,610 --> 00:03:32,390
Okay, so it is constantly managed.

55
00:03:32,670 --> 00:03:32,870
Yes.

56
00:03:33,690 --> 00:03:41,290
And the ultimate takeaway here is that writing down this agreed menu is what lets twenty separate agencies behave as one single framework

57
00:03:41,710 --> 00:03:43,770
rather than twenty incompatible projects.

